# section s89
# Used by the step that must leave Quick actions visible. Scrolling down toward
# it does not work: the row enters the accessibility tree while still off-screen
# and settles at y=-20, just above the top edge, so "scroll until visible" never
# becomes true and a tap on its clamped centre hits the status bar -- which on
# iOS scrolls the list to the top and loses it outright.
#
# Measured on this runtime: at the bottom of Settings the row is at y=-20, and
# one `scroll up --pixels 60` puts it at y=414. `--pixels` is a gesture
# magnitude rather than a literal delta, so 96 and above carry it straight back
# out of the tree.
scroll bottom
wait stable 400 5000
scroll up --pixels 60

# section s89-android
# Android's own calibration of the step above, kept apart rather than merged.
# `scroll up --pixels 60` is a flick, and its magnitude does not mean the same
# thing on both runtimes: on iOS it lands the row at y=414, on Android it leaves
# it at y=32..198 -- overlapping the app's floating header (y=158..279) and
# hard against the status bar, so the tap that follows opens nothing and the
# flow fails two steps later looking for a sheet that never came.
#
# `gesture pan` is a drag rather than a flick, so its delta is the delta.
# Measured here on a Pixel 8 emulator at 1080x2400: from the bottom of
# Settings, panning down 400 moves the row from y=32..198 to y=403..569, which
# clears the header with room to spare.
scroll bottom
wait stable 400 5000
gesture pan 540 800 0 400 400
wait stable 400 5000

# section clear-native-header
# Settle only. This used to scroll up to lift the row clear of the native
# large-title header, back when the step above overshot and left it at y=-20.
# That step is now a measured `scroll bottom` plus one small nudge, so the row
# arrives where it is reachable and any further scrolling here only carries it
# straight back out of the tree.
wait stable 400 5000

